-- allium: 3
-- observability.allium
--
-- Scope: Observability — per-task audit log of MCP tool calls, feature-usage
--        recording for TUI keybindings, slow-DB-call latency warnings, the
--        decode-failure policy for rows read back from the database, and the
--        `dispatch statusline` process that records each session's
--        subscription windows.
-- Includes: TrajectoryEntry value type, append-on-each-MCP-call rule,
--           fire-and-forget error handling;
--           FeatureUsageEvent value type and the rules deciding when a
--           keypress is recorded (every keypress that takes effect, once),
--           when it is not (a keypress that changes nothing), and how the
--           event log stays bounded (oldest-first eviction at a row cap);
--           SlowDbCallWarning value type and the DbCallSlowWarning rule that
--           emits a warn-level log line when a DB call exceeds the configured
--           latency threshold, on either dispatch path (the single writer
--           connection or a read-only pool connection);
--           DecodeFallbackWarning value type and the three rules deciding what
--           an undecodable row costs: bulk reads skip it and warn, single-entity
--           reads fail, and errors that are not row-decode failures always
--           propagate;
--           StatusLineDecorator, the `dispatch statusline` process wired as
--           the statusLine command of every dispatch-spawned session — it
--           records the subscription windows from the payload Claude Code
--           hands it, and stands in for the status line the operator had
--           before dispatch took the slot.
-- Excludes: tmux pane capture, Claude lifecycle hooks (PreToolUse, Stop —
--           see agent-health.allium), dispatch-internal commands, reader UIs,
--           replay tooling. The board readout of the windows
--           StatusLineDecorator records is one such reader UI (see
--           dispatch.allium: TokenBudgetIndicator).
--           Reading recorded usage back out belongs to mcp-task-tools.allium
--           (QueryUsageViaMcp); the keybinding-to-action mapping itself
--           belongs to the spec owning each surface (core, tasks, epics,
--           split-pane, todo, …), not here.
--           Slow-db-call warnings reach a durable log file in one process:
--           the TUI, which initialises a tracing subscriber writing to
--           <data_dir>/app.log. Hooks used to be the second such process;
--           they no longer open a database at all (see agent-health.allium:
--           HookDelivery), so the calls they once made are now the board's
--           own and are logged there. The one-shot CLI subcommands (update,
--           pr-gate, list, repo, prune-repo-paths, plan, setup) call the same
--           shared DB-call paths, but with no subscriber configured their
--           warn events are silently dropped -- persisting those is out of
--           scope for now (see DbCallSlowWarning for the scope boundary).

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A single line in <dispatch_data_dir>/trajectories/<task_id>.jsonl.
-- Each MCP tool call from a dispatched-agent caller becomes one
-- TrajectoryEntry, serialised as a single JSON object on its own line.
-- Entries for a given task accumulate in a single per-task file named
-- by the task's integer ID. The centralized data dir (parent of the
-- SQLite database) holds all trajectory files so they outlive worktree
-- cleanup and remain queryable in a single location.
--
-- args and result are stored as opaque JSON strings to keep this spec free
-- of MCP payload shape; the schema_version field lets readers detect format
-- evolution.
value TrajectoryEntry {
    schema_version: String     -- semver string, e.g. "1.0.0"
    timestamp:      Timestamp  -- UTC, RFC 3339
    task_id:        String     -- stringified Task.id of the caller
    method:         String     -- MCP tool name (e.g. "update_task")
    args:           String     -- full tool arguments as received, JSON-encoded
    result:         String     -- response payload on success (JSON-encoded);
                               -- on failure, the JSON object {"error": "<msg>"}
    duration_ms:    Integer    -- wall-clock time from dispatch to response
}

-- A single warn-level log record emitted when an async DB call takes longer
-- than the configured latency threshold. Unlike TrajectoryEntry, this is not
-- persisted to a dedicated file: it is a tracing event whose durable capture
-- depends on the running process having a subscriber configured (see
-- DbCallSlowWarning for the scope boundary). It has no identity and no lifecycle.
value SlowDbCallWarning {
    duration_ms: Integer   -- measured wall-clock elapsed time of the DB call,
                           -- including time spent queued behind other calls on
                           -- the same connection, or retrying behind another
                           -- connection's SQLite lock
    execute_ms:  Integer   -- how long the call's own work ran once it held a
                           -- connection. The directly measured part
    queued_ms:   Integer   -- the rest of duration_ms: everything that was not
                           -- the call's own work, which is dominated by waiting
                           -- for a connection but also absorbs dispatch
                           -- overhead. Derived as duration_ms - execute_ms
                           -- rather than measured, so the two parts always sum
                           -- to duration_ms exactly
    location:    String    -- call-site of the invoking db_call / db_call_read
                           -- caller, formatted as "file.rs:line:column"
                           -- (std::panic::Location's Display, which includes
                           -- the column)
}

-- A single warn-level log record emitted when a row read back from the
-- database cannot be decoded into its domain type, or when an unknown enum
-- value is defaulted rather than failing the row. Like SlowDbCallWarning this
-- is a tracing event, not a persisted entity: no identity, no lifecycle. Its
-- durable capture depends on the running process having a subscriber
-- configured (same scope boundary as DbCallSlowWarning).
value DecodeFallbackWarning {
    entity_kind: String   -- which collection the row came from, e.g. "tasks"
    reason:      String   -- the decode failure, verbatim
    count:       Integer  -- running total of decode fallbacks in this process,
                          -- monotonic from process start and never reset, so
                          -- readers compare deltas rather than absolutes
}

-- One recorded use of a countable feature. Unlike the two warning values
-- above this one is persisted, and unlike TrajectoryEntry it is aggregated
-- rather than read back individually: the only consumer counts and dates
-- them per (category, action, detail, actor) group. It has no identity — two
-- uses of the same key are two indistinguishable events, and that is the
-- point.
value FeatureUsageEvent {
    category: UsageCategory
    action:   String          -- what the use did, e.g. "navigate_row",
                              -- named after the effect rather than the key,
                              -- so a rebound key keeps its history
    detail:   String?         -- the key as typed, e.g. "j", " ", "Enter",
                              -- distinguishing which binding drove a shared
                              -- action
    actor:    UsageActor
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum UsageCategory { keybinding | mcp_tool }

enum UsageActor { human | agent }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    trajectory_schema_version: String = "1.0.0"
    -- Subdirectory name within the dispatch data dir that holds trajectory files.
    trajectory_subdir: String = "trajectories"
    -- Maximum number of feature-usage events retained. Recording an event
    -- when the log is already at the cap evicts the oldest events, so the log
    -- is a bounded sliding window rather than an ever-growing table. 100_000
    -- is several months of recording at observed rates, which is the horizon
    -- pruning decisions are taken over.
    usage_event_row_cap: Integer = 100_000
    -- A DB call whose measured wall-clock elapsed time exceeds this many
    -- milliseconds is considered abnormally slow and triggers a warn log.
    -- The same threshold applies on both dispatch paths (writer and read pool).
    -- 200ms sits well above normal SQLite query time (sub-millisecond to low
    -- milliseconds) and well below perceptible user lag, so it only fires on
    -- genuinely abnormal calls.
    slow_db_call_threshold_ms: Integer = 200
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Append on MCP tool call ==

rule TrajectoryAppend {
    -- After every dispatched-agent MCP tool call, append a TrajectoryEntry
    -- to the per-task trajectory file in the centralized dispatch data dir.
    -- Session callers produce no entry.
    when: McpToolCall(caller_identity, method, args, result, duration_ms)

    -- Only Task-kind callers are logged. Session callers are silently dropped.
    requires: caller_identity.kind = task

    -- The caller's task must exist in the DB. Entries are written regardless
    -- of whether the task has a worktree — the write path is independent of
    -- the worktree field.
    requires: core/Task.exists(caller_identity.task_id)

    ensures: append_jsonl(
        path: dispatch_data_dir
              + "/" + config.trajectory_subdir
              + "/" + str(caller_identity.task_id) + ".jsonl",
        entry: TrajectoryEntry {
            schema_version: config.trajectory_schema_version,
            timestamp: now,
            task_id: str(caller_identity.task_id),
            method: method,
            args: args,
            result: result,
            duration_ms: duration_ms
        }
    )

    @guidance
        -- dispatch_data_dir is the parent directory of the SQLite database
        -- (e.g. ~/.local/share/dispatch/). It is not task-specific.
        -- append_jsonl opens the file in append mode (O_APPEND | O_CREAT),
        -- writes one JSON-encoded TrajectoryEntry followed by "\n", flushes,
        -- and closes the file. The flush is required, not incidental: the
        -- underlying async file write can report success while the bytes
        -- are still in-process and not yet visible to a subsequent
        -- open/read of the same path — an un-awaited flush would let entries
        -- disappear under concurrent load with no error anywhere. The
        -- trajectories/ subdirectory is created with
        -- create_dir_all if absent — unlike the old worktree-relative path,
        -- the parent directory is auto-created so there is no noop-on-missing-
        -- dir behaviour.
        -- Implementation lives behind the MCP handler dispatch boundary so
        -- that every tool call (create_task, update_task, query_learnings,
        -- record_learning, query_usage, subscribe_to_task, dispatch_task,
        -- list_tasks, list_epics, …) is captured uniformly. A native
        -- SendMessage call between agents never reaches this boundary at
        -- all — see HookPeerMessageSent in agent-health.allium for how that
        -- gets its own trajectory entry instead.
        -- The duration_ms field measures the wall-clock interval from the
        -- moment the handler receives the parsed JSON-RPC request to the
        -- moment it returns a response (success or error). The timestamp
        -- field records the start of the call (before dispatch_tool runs),
        -- not the time of writing.
        -- For failed handler invocations, the result string is the JSON
        -- object {"error": "<msg>"} where <msg> is the JSON-RPC error
        -- message; the duration_ms still reflects time-to-error.
}

-- == Fire-and-forget error handling ==

rule TrajectoryWriteFailureIsSilent {
    -- Trajectory writes are best-effort. File I/O errors (permission denied,
    -- disk full, …) must not propagate to the calling MCP handler or affect
    -- the handler's response.
    when: TrajectoryWriteFailed(task_id, error)

    ensures: TrajectoryWriteWarning(task_id: task_id, error: error)

    @guidance
        -- The implementation wraps the append in a fallible block; on error
        -- the error is forwarded to tracing::warn (target: "trajectory")
        -- with the task_id and error message, and the handler proceeds to
        -- return its normal response. There is no retry, no buffering, no
        -- dead-letter file. A flush failure after a successful write is
        -- warned the same way as a write failure — the entry is still lost,
        -- and the caller is still not told.
        -- TrajectoryWriteWarning is observable via the standard tracing
        -- subscriber; it has no entity representation in this spec.
}

rule AlreadyDoneTeardownIsNotAFailure {
    -- A best-effort teardown whose target has already gone did not fail: it
    -- found the state it was asking for. Killing a tmux window that has already
    -- closed, or clearing tracking entries for a task that has already been
    -- deleted, both land here.
    --
    -- Reported at debug rather than warn. The distinction is not cosmetic: a
    -- teardown warning that fires on the normal case trains the reader to skip
    -- the line, which is how the genuinely-failed teardowns beside it stop being
    -- read too.
    when: TeardownTargetAlreadyGone(target)

    ensures: TeardownSkipNoted(target: target)

    @guidance
        -- The distinction must be drawn on WHY the teardown did not happen, not
        -- on the fact that it did not. A teardown that was attempted and failed
        -- leaves a real window or real rows behind and still warns — see
        -- TeardownFailureIsWarned below.
        --
        -- Deciding which case applies needs a signal narrower than "an error
        -- came back", and each call site has one: task-clearing gets a typed
        -- not-found error from the service layer, while the tmux kill has only
        -- the resolver's own message. Matching on that message is sound because
        -- dispatch produces it itself and a test pins the wording — it would not
        -- be sound for an external tool's output.
}

rule TeardownFailureIsWarned {
    -- The other direction of AlreadyDoneTeardownIsNotAFailure: a teardown that
    -- was actually attempted and failed. The window is still there, or the rows
    -- are, and nothing will retry — so this is exactly the case the warning
    -- exists for.
    when: TeardownAttemptFailed(target, error)

    ensures: TeardownWarning(target: target, error: error)

    @guidance
        -- Still best-effort in the sense that no caller is told and nothing is
        -- retried: the surrounding operation has already persisted, and there is
        -- no recovery a caller could take. The log line is the entire response.
}

rule UsageWriteFailureIsSilent {
    -- Feature-usage writes are best-effort in the same sense trajectory
    -- writes are: the failure must not propagate to the keypress handler or
    -- the MCP handler that triggered it, and must not be retried. It must,
    -- however, be reported to the log. Discarding the write's error outright
    -- would make a lost count indistinguishable from a feature nobody used —
    -- and since pruning reads the absence of a count as "unused", that
    -- silence can retire a binding that is in daily use.
    when: FeatureUsageWriteFailed(event, error)

    ensures: UsageWriteWarning(event: event, error: error)

    @guidance
        -- Both fire-and-forget call sites — the TUI's UsageCommand::Record
        -- command and the MCP handler's post-dispatch recording — route
        -- through one helper, src/service/usage.rs::record_usage_event_logged,
        -- rather than each writing its own error branch. The helper awaits
        -- the write and, on error, emits a warn-level line (target "usage")
        -- carrying the event's category, its action, and the error message;
        -- the action is what makes the warning attributable to a specific
        -- counter. The helper does not spawn: callers keep ownership of the
        -- fire-and-forget shape, so the write stays off the critical path
        -- and no caller newly awaits telemetry.
        -- UsageWriteWarning is observable via the standard tracing
        -- subscriber; it has no entity representation in this spec.
}

-- == Feature usage recording ==

rule KeypressRecordsFeatureUsage {
    -- Every keypress the TUI acts on records exactly one keybinding usage
    -- event, naming the effect it had and the key that produced it. This is
    -- the evidence base for deciding which bindings earn their place, so the
    -- obligation is on the surface, not on a chosen subset of it: board
    -- navigation, the task-detail and TODO overlays, the archive column,
    -- every confirmation dialog and every picker all record, exactly as the
    -- action keys do.
    when: KeypressHandled(action, key, took_effect)

    -- A keypress that changes nothing records nothing. Counting inert
    -- keypresses would report a binding as used precisely when it is failing
    -- to do anything, which inverts the signal this data exists to give:
    -- pressing `e` with no card selected, `U` on an unlinked todo, or a key
    -- that no arm claims at all leaves no trace.
    requires: took_effect

    -- Characters typed into a text field are data entry, not use of a
    -- binding: recording them would drown every real action in keystroke
    -- noise and answer a question nobody is asking. A text mode is recorded
    -- once, when it is committed or abandoned.
    requires: not is_text_entry(key)

    ensures: FeatureUsageRecorded(
        event: FeatureUsageEvent {
            category: keybinding,
            action: action,
            detail: key,
            actor: human
        }
    )

    @guidance
        -- One press, one event: a key whose handler fans out into several
        -- commands still records once, and the count therefore reads as
        -- "times pressed", not "work done".
        -- action names the effect, not the key: two bindings for the same
        -- effect share an action and are told apart by detail, and rebinding
        -- a key preserves the action's history across the rename. That is
        -- also why the count for an action retired and later reused under the
        -- same name needs a `since` window to read correctly.
        -- "Took effect" is judged by whether the handler produced any command
        -- or state change, not by whether the user could see it: a key that
        -- opens a dialog, moves a cursor, or dispatches an agent all count
        -- equally. In the implementation this is the dispatch_keyed /
        -- dispatch_handler_keyed pair in src/tui/input.rs — the latter only
        -- records when the sub-handler returned a non-empty command list,
        -- which is what keeps inert presses silent.
        -- is_text_entry covers the printable characters, backspace and caret
        -- motions of a text field, in every mode that has one — the task and
        -- epic title/description fields, the repo-path, base-branch, todo
        -- and preset-name fields, the board search query and the
        -- quick-dispatch filter. Their surrounding actions are
        -- still recorded: the key that opened the field, the cursor moves in
        -- a field that also drives a candidate list, and the submit or cancel
        -- that closes it.
        -- Recording is best-effort and off the critical path: the event is
        -- written asynchronously and a failed write is not retried, though it
        -- is logged — see UsageWriteFailureIsSilent. A dropped event costs one
        -- count; blocking the key handler on the database would cost the user
        -- their keystroke.
        -- Agent-driven MCP tool calls record into the same log under the
        -- mcp_tool category, so the two are queried together but never
        -- conflated: category and actor both separate them.
}

rule FeatureUsageLogStaysBounded {
    -- Recording is unconditional and uncapped at the call site, so the log
    -- itself has to be the thing that stays bounded: retaining the most
    -- recent config.usage_event_row_cap events and dropping older ones keeps
    -- an always-on counter from growing without limit. Eviction is by age,
    -- not by category, so no feature's history is protected from a noisier
    -- one — a burst of navigation shortens the window for everything.
    when: FeatureUsageRecorded(event)

    ensures: OldestFeatureUsageEventsEvicted(
        retained: config.usage_event_row_cap
    )

    @guidance
        -- Enforced on every insert rather than by a periodic sweep, so the
        -- bound holds continuously and no separate maintenance path can be
        -- forgotten or fail. Below the cap the eviction is a no-op.
        -- The consequence worth stating: counts are only complete within the
        -- retained window. A total read from this log is a lower bound on
        -- all-time use, which is why pruning decisions window by date rather
        -- than trusting an all-time total.
}

-- == Slow DB call latency warning ==

rule DbCallSlowWarning {
    -- When an async call to the SQLite database completes, its wall-clock
    -- elapsed time is measured from just before the call is issued to when it
    -- resolves — which includes any time the call spent queued behind other
    -- calls on the same connection, or retrying behind another connection's
    -- write lock. If that elapsed time exceeds the configured threshold, a
    -- warn-level log line is emitted carrying the elapsed duration and the
    -- call site that issued the call.
    --
    -- "db_call" in this rule's name and prose means either dispatch path: the
    -- single writer connection (db_call, which serves every mutation) or one of
    -- the read-only pool connections (db_call_read, which serves pure reads).
    -- DbCallCompleted fires for both, and the threshold check and resulting
    -- warning are identical on both. Which connection served the call is not
    -- part of the warning.
    when: DbCallCompleted(location, elapsed_ms, execute_ms)

    -- Only abnormally slow calls warn. Calls at or below the threshold produce
    -- no log line. The threshold is deliberately still measured against total
    -- elapsed time, not execute_ms: a call that really did take five seconds
    -- mattered to whoever was waiting on it, however the time was spent. The
    -- split below changes what the warning explains, not which calls it fires
    -- for.
    requires: elapsed_ms > config.slow_db_call_threshold_ms

    ensures: SlowDbCallLogged(
        warning: SlowDbCallWarning {
            duration_ms: elapsed_ms,
            queued_ms: elapsed_ms - execute_ms,
            execute_ms: execute_ms,
            location: location
        }
    )

    @guidance
        -- The elapsed time is real wall-clock time (std::time::Instant), not an
        -- injected clock: this is pure observability, not domain logic under
        -- test with fake time. location is std::panic::Location::caller() of
        -- whichever db_call call site was invoked, obtained for free via
        -- #[track_caller] so no call site needs to change; it is rendered via
        -- std::panic::Location's Display as "file.rs:line:column".
        --
        -- WHY THE DURATION IS SPLIT: read calls are assigned to a pool
        -- connection round-robin, so a cheap query can land on a connection
        -- that is already mid-flight on an unrelated slow read, wait out that
        -- connection's queue, and cross the threshold on queueing alone. The
        -- single-connection writer path has always had the same ambiguity (a
        -- fast call queued behind a slow one), now spread across several
        -- connections instead of one.
        --
        -- Reporting only total elapsed time made the warning name the victim
        -- rather than the cause, and misleadingly: the most frequent call site
        -- in a real log was `task_exists`, a SELECT 1 on the primary key, at
        -- 200-300ms. A single-row indexed lookup cannot cost that, so the
        -- location was pointing at an innocent query every time.
        --
        -- queued_ms and execute_ms make the two separable from a single line,
        -- with no timestamp correlation across neighbouring warnings. High
        -- queued_ms with low execute_ms means contention and the location is
        -- incidental; high execute_ms means the logged call site really is the
        -- expensive one.
        --
        -- SCOPE BOUNDARY (intentional, not a bug): SlowDbCallLogged is a
        -- tracing::warn event ("slow db_call"), not an entity, and its durable
        -- capture depends on the running process having a tracing subscriber.
        -- One process initialises a subscriber and thus persists these
        -- warnings to <data_dir>/app.log: the TUI subcommand. Both dispatch
        -- paths are shared code, so the timing check runs in every process
        -- that calls either of them (the TUI, its in-process MCP server and
        -- FeedRunner, and each one-shot CLI subcommand), but only the TUI has
        -- a subscriber, so only its warnings are persisted. The `hook`
        -- subcommand used to be the second, because it fired on every agent
        -- tool-use event and was the likeliest source of the write-lock
        -- contention this instrumentation exists to catch. That contention is
        -- gone: a hook opens no database (see agent-health.allium:
        -- HookDelivery), and the work it used to do in its own process now
        -- runs inside the board's, under the board's subscriber — so the
        -- calls this warning was added for are still logged, by the process
        -- that now makes them.
        -- The remaining one-shot subcommands (update, pr-gate, list, repo,
        -- prune-repo-paths, plan, setup) still have no subscriber, so their
        -- warn events are silently dropped; persisting those is deferred.
        --
        -- No new metric, counter, or queryable surface: the log line in the
        -- existing app.log is the complete deliverable.
}

-- == Decode-failure policy ==

rule BulkReadSkipsUndecodableRow {
    -- A read that answers "all the tasks/epics matching X" must stay useful
    -- when one stored row cannot be decoded. The offending row is omitted from
    -- the result, every other row is returned, and the omission is warned.
    -- Blanking the whole board because one row is corrupt is not an acceptable
    -- failure mode: the corrupt row is one card, the board is the product.
    when: BulkRowDecodeFailed(entity_kind, reason, fallback_total)

    ensures:
        RowOmittedFromBulkResult(entity_kind: entity_kind)
        DecodeFallbackLogged(
            warning: DecodeFallbackWarning {
                entity_kind: entity_kind,
                reason: reason,
                count: fallback_total
            }
        )

    @guidance
        -- "Bulk read" means any read whose contract is a collection: the task
        -- board load, the per-status column loads, the epic lists, and the
        -- per-epic task lists. Skipping is applied once, at the point the row
        -- iterator is collected, rather than in each field decoder — so a row
        -- that fails for any reason (unknown status, unknown sub-status, an
        -- unparseable timestamp, an inconsistent url/url_type pair) is handled
        -- identically.
        -- fallback_total is the value of the process-wide counter *after* this
        -- fallback, exposed for tests and ad-hoc debugging so a slow bleed of
        -- skipped rows is detectable without chasing log lines. It is bumped
        -- eagerly, before the warn event is constructed: a counter incremented
        -- as a log field value would stop counting entirely in processes with
        -- no tracing subscriber.
        -- The same counter covers field-level fallbacks (an unknown feed_role
        -- or epic origin defaulting rather than failing its row), so the total
        -- reads as "decode surprises seen", not "rows dropped".
}

rule SingleEntityReadFailsOnUndecodableRow {
    -- A read that names one specific row reports the decode failure instead of
    -- answering "not found". The caller asked for that row: pretending it does
    -- not exist would turn a corrupt row into a silent absence, and would
    -- remove the only remaining way to diagnose a row the bulk reads have
    -- stopped surfacing.
    when: SingleRowDecodeFailed(reason)

    ensures: ReadFailed(reason: reason)

    @guidance
        -- Covers reads keyed to one row: fetching a task or epic by id, and
        -- finding the task owning a given plan path. Deliberately asymmetric
        -- with BulkReadSkipsUndecodableRow — the two rules answer different
        -- questions, so they earn different failure modes.
}

rule QueryFailurePropagatesFromBulkRead {
    -- Only row-content failures are skippable. An error raised by the query
    -- itself — I/O failure, interrupt, or a mismatch between the selected
    -- columns and the decoder — fails the whole read.
    when: BulkQueryFailed(reason)

    ensures: ReadFailed(reason: reason)

    @guidance
        -- The distinction matters because the two error classes look alike at
        -- the row iterator: both arrive as a failed row. Treating a mid-iteration
        -- I/O error as one bad row would silently truncate an otherwise healthy
        -- result set — the caller would see a short list and no error, which is
        -- strictly worse than the blank board this policy exists to prevent.
        -- A column-name mismatch is likewise a code defect, not data corruption,
        -- and must not be absorbed into a warn line.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every persisted TrajectoryEntry must carry the current schema version.
-- Readers use this field to dispatch between format versions; an entry
-- without a schema_version is unreadable.
invariant TrajectoryEntryHasSchemaVersion {
    for e in TrajectoryEntries:
        e.schema_version != ""
}

-- A trajectory entry's task_id must identify a real task. Entries are only
-- ever produced by TrajectoryAppend, which guards on Task.exists, so this
-- holds by construction.
invariant TrajectoryEntryReferencesRealTask {
    for e in TrajectoryEntries:
        core/Task.exists(e.task_id)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface StatusLineDecorator {
    facing operator: User
    -- prose: `dispatch statusline --snapshot <path> [--chain <command>]`, the
    -- process that produces the data dispatch.allium's TokenBudgetIndicator reads. It is wired as
    -- the statusLine command of every dispatch-spawned Claude session, so it
    -- runs several times a second in every session, for the whole life of every
    -- session. That frequency, and the fact that a failure here breaks the
    -- operator's status line rather than anything of ours, is what every
    -- guarantee below is about.
    --
    -- It has exactly two jobs: record the subscription windows from the payload
    -- Claude Code hands it on stdin, and stand in for the status line the
    -- operator had before dispatch took the slot over.

    -- Whether the payload carried subscription windows to publish.
    let payload_reports_windows = status_payload_has_rate_limits()

    -- The command the decorator stands in for, if any. Absent when the operator
    -- had no status line, or when theirs is itself a dispatch statusline
    -- invocation (see NeverChainsToItself).
    let chained_command = discovered_chain()

    exposes:
        payload_reports_windows
        chained_command

    @guarantee AlwaysSucceeds
        -- Every path exits successfully and prints at most an empty status
        -- line: an unparseable payload, a payload without subscription windows,
        -- an unwritable snapshot location, a chained command that is missing,
        -- fails, or outlives its budget. A blank status line is the worst
        -- outcome the operator can be shown; a broken one is not available.

    @guarantee NeverReadsOrWritesTheDatabase
        -- The decorator never touches the task database — not to read it, and
        -- not by bringing it into existence. At this invocation rate, across
        -- every session, database work here would be pure waste.

    @guarantee StartsNoAsyncRuntime
        -- The decorator does no asynchronous work, and starts none of the
        -- machinery for it: no worker-thread pool, no reactor. At several
        -- invocations a second in every session, concurrently, setup it cannot
        -- use is the same waste as database work (see
        -- NeverReadsOrWritesTheDatabase).

    @guarantee PublishedSnapshotIsAlwaysWholeAndFromOneWriter
        -- Every session publishes to the same snapshot location concurrently. A
        -- reader therefore either sees no snapshot or sees one complete set of
        -- windows written by a single session — never a blend of two writers'
        -- values, never a partially written one. The last write wins, which is
        -- correct because the windows are account-global: every session reports
        -- the same figures. Publishing also leaves nothing behind but the
        -- snapshot itself.

    @guarantee SnapshotIndependentOfChain
        -- The snapshot is published whatever the chained command does, and
        -- before waiting on it. The budget readout never depends on a status
        -- line the operator configured for unrelated reasons.

    @guarantee ChainedOutputReproducedVerbatim
        -- With a chained command, the payload reaches it unchanged and its
        -- output is reproduced unchanged, whatever the payload's size. The
        -- operator's status line looks exactly as it did before dispatch
        -- occupied the slot.

    @guarantee ChainedCommandIsBounded
        -- One budget of config.statusline_chain_timeout covers the chained
        -- command's output AND its exit. A command that never produces output
        -- and one that produces output but never exits are both abandoned at
        -- the deadline and leave nothing running behind them. Neither can hold
        -- the decorator — and so Claude Code's status line — open.
        --
        -- An abandoned command contributes nothing: output it had already
        -- produced is discarded along with it, and the operator sees the same
        -- empty status line as for any other failure. A partial status line is
        -- indistinguishable from a complete one, so showing half of what the
        -- operator's own command meant to say would be a worse outcome than
        -- showing none of it.

    @guarantee SnapshotLocationIsFixedNotDerivedFromTheOpenDatabase
        -- The subscription windows are account-global, so the snapshot has
        -- exactly one location per machine. Both sides agree on it
        -- unconditionally: the location the decorator is told to publish to,
        -- and the location dispatch.allium's TokenBudgetIndicator reads. Neither varies with
        -- which task database the process that computed it happened to be
        -- running against.
        --
        -- The decorator's settings are written by whichever dispatch process
        -- notices they are missing or wrong, and they then govern every Claude
        -- session started afterwards. Deriving the location from the open
        -- database instead let one run against a throwaway database — a
        -- developer's scratch database, or a test that boots the runtime
        -- against a temporary one — silently repoint every later session at a
        -- directory belonging to that run. Sessions kept publishing, the
        -- indicator kept reading its own unchanged location, and the result
        -- was a permanently stale readout with no failure reported anywhere:
        -- indistinguishable, to the operator, from an account that had simply
        -- stopped reporting.

    @guarantee SettingsLocationIsAnExplicitStartupInput
        -- Startup is told where the operator's Claude Code configuration
        -- lives — the directory the decorator's settings file goes in, and
        -- the trust store beside it. It goes looking for neither. The
        -- operator's own session is handed the operator's locations. A run
        -- that is not the operator's session — a test booting the runtime —
        -- is handed locations of its own, and so cannot reach theirs.
        --
        -- One resolved set of locations rather than a parameter per file, so
        -- the next operator-owned path startup learns about is added where
        -- the others already are, and cannot arrive as a fresh lookup.
        --
        -- Resolving the directory inside startup instead made every run that
        -- boots the runtime a writer of the operator's real configuration,
        -- whatever else that run was for. The written content happened to be
        -- machine-correct, so the rewrite showed up nowhere; the next key
        -- baked into that file need not be. Unlike
        -- SnapshotLocationIsFixedNotDerivedFromTheOpenDatabase, no path is
        -- derived from anything here — the lookup itself is the hazard, so
        -- being handed the directory is the whole of the fix.

    @guarantee AnUnavailableHomeDirectoryIsAFailureNotAPath
        -- Every operator-owned location startup is handed is found under the
        -- home directory, and each is resolved from one reading of it. When
        -- that reading yields nothing usable, resolution reports that and
        -- startup stops. It never yields a location anyway.
        --
        -- Two locations sit beside each other there — the configuration
        -- directory, and the trust store next to it — and they are resolved
        -- the same way, because they are the same kind of thing. A resolution
        -- that failed for one and quietly succeeded for the other would leave
        -- which of the two a run consulted deciding whether the operator was
        -- told anything at all.
        --
        -- The hazard is specific to the answer being a path. An absent home
        -- directory composes into a perfectly well-formed location — one at
        -- the root of the filesystem, or one relative to whatever directory
        -- the process happens to be running in — and everything downstream
        -- then treats it as the operator's own. A trust store read there holds
        -- no record of any repository, so every repository reads as untrusted;
        -- the operator sees dispatch declining to launch, with nothing naming
        -- the home directory anywhere. Granting trust would write the file
        -- into existence, at that location, and the next read would find it.
        -- Nothing in that sequence is an error, which is why the resolution
        -- has to be the thing that refuses.
        --
        -- An empty home directory counts as unavailable, not as the root: a
        -- shell spells "unset" both by omitting the variable and by setting it
        -- to nothing, and only one of those spellings failing is the same
        -- split again, one level down.

    @guarantee SpawnSitesAndStartupNameTheSameConfigurationDirectory
        -- Dispatch owns two things inside the operator's Claude Code
        -- configuration directory: the decorator's settings file, and the
        -- directory holding dispatch's own agent-facing plugin. Each has
        -- exactly one location, and both halves of the system agree on it.
        -- The directory the writing side is handed is the same directory
        -- every spawned session is pointed at when it is launched.
        --
        -- The writing side is two flows, not one: startup keeps the settings
        -- file current, and installation puts the plugin in place. Neither
        -- looks the directory up; each is handed it (see
        -- SettingsLocationIsAnExplicitStartupInput). The reading side is the
        -- text that launches a session, which names both locations outright,
        -- identically for every session, because that text is assembled once
        -- and is not free to vary per run.
        --
        -- Those two mechanisms stay separate, but they are not two
        -- definitions. The names of the directory and of what sits inside it
        -- are written down once, and both halves are built from that one
        -- statement — the launch text by embedding it, the writing side by
        -- appending it to a resolved location. Renaming any of them is
        -- therefore a single edit that necessarily moves both halves at once:
        -- neither half holds a copy the edit could miss. Both are still
        -- pinned, separately, to expectations written out by hand, so a
        -- rename fails visibly on each side rather than quietly carrying
        -- through. Agent-facing copy that merely quotes a location for a human
        -- to read stands outside this — it is prose, not a half of the
        -- agreement, and nothing is launched from it.
        --
        -- What is left over, and it is one thing. The launch text names the
        -- directory relative to the home directory, and a shell resolves it
        -- there. The agreement therefore also needs the configuration
        -- directory to be found under the home directory — which is not a
        -- name, so it is not covered by the shared statement above, and is
        -- asserted instead. That assertion has a limit worth stating plainly:
        -- a lookup which consulted some further input alongside the home
        -- directory would keep matching everywhere that input was absent,
        -- which is everywhere the assertion runs, and part only for the
        -- operators who set it. So making the directory operator-configurable
        -- is a change that must move both halves in the same commit and give
        -- up naming the location outright. Nothing can catch that one after
        -- the fact, and no structure prevents it either: the lookup is one
        -- small function, and a second input is a short edit inside it. What
        -- stands there instead is a written warning at that exact spot. That
        -- is weaker than everything else in this guarantee, and is said
        -- plainly so nobody mistakes it for more.
        --
        -- Leaving the agreement to hold by coincidence meant a change on
        -- either side split the two in silence: a settings file written where
        -- no session ever loaded it, a plugin installed where no session ever
        -- found it, and every session launched against a location nothing had
        -- written. A session refuses to start at all without that settings
        -- file, so every dispatch failed at the moment of spawning, and
        -- nothing anywhere named the cause. The failure is total and says
        -- nothing about its own origin, which is why the agreement is worth
        -- making structural rather than assuming.

    @guarantee NeverChainsToItself
        -- When the status line the decorator would stand in for is itself a
        -- dispatch statusline invocation, nothing is chained. An empty status
        -- line with the windows still being recorded is the honest outcome;
        -- recursion is not.

    @guidance
        -- Implemented by src/cli/statusline.rs. The command string, including
        -- the discovered chain and the quoted snapshot location, is generated by
        -- src/setup/statusline.rs into a dispatch-owned settings file that every
        -- spawn site passes to `claude` — deliberately never the operator's own
        -- settings file. src/runtime/mod.rs rewrites that file at startup
        -- whenever its content differs from what the current build would
        -- write — absence is only the commonest such difference, and `claude`
        -- refuses to start without it. The locations it writes into arrive as
        -- `StartupPaths`, resolved once in src/main.rs; the same shape
        -- src/setup/mod.rs already uses for setup and uninstall. The path
        -- segments are stated once, in src/claude_paths.rs, as macros rather
        -- than consts because the reading side assembles them with concat!,
        -- which takes literals and macro expansions but not a const item. The
        -- reader is src/dispatch/prompts.rs::DISPATCH_PLUGIN_DIR; the writers
        -- are src/setup/mod.rs::claude_dir,
        -- src/setup/statusline.rs::settings_path and
        -- src/setup/plugins.rs::plugin_dir_under. Both halves are pinned to
        -- hand-written expectations — src/dispatch/tests.rs for the constant,
        -- src/setup/mod.rs's own tests for the layout, which also carry the
        -- home-directory link. Each writer's resolution is pinned too:
        -- `StartupPaths::resolve` in src/runtime/tests/misc.rs,
        -- `SetupPaths::resolve` in src/setup/mod.rs's tests.
        -- src/main.rs::cmd_tui needs no test: `StartupPaths`' fields are
        -- private to src/runtime/mod.rs, so `resolve` is the only way to
        -- obtain one.
        --
        -- AnUnavailableHomeDirectoryIsAFailureNotAPath is
        -- src/setup/mod.rs::home_dir, the one reader of `$HOME` behind both
        -- src/setup/mod.rs::claude_dir and
        -- src/setup/mod.rs::user_global_config_path. Startup reads it once,
        -- in `StartupPaths::resolve`, and composes both locations from that
        -- one reading. The joining half of each is a separate function taking
        -- the home directory as an argument (`claude_dir_in`,
        -- `user_global_config_path_in`), so a test can vary it — comparing two
        -- locations both derived from the real `$HOME` cancels it out of both
        -- sides and asserts nothing. The same shape as
        -- src/editor.rs::resolve_editor, and for the same reason:
        -- `std::env::set_var` is unsafe in edition 2024 and races the test
        -- harness's threads regardless.
        --
        -- The empty-is-unavailable rule reaches past the locations startup is
        -- handed, because the same spelling composes just as quietly
        -- elsewhere: src/tmux.rs::tmux_conf_path resolves through the same
        -- reader, and src/models/paths.rs::expand_tilde and
        -- src/lib.rs::default_db_path apply the rule to their own fallbacks —
        -- neither has a Result to report an absence through, so each stays
        -- put instead. expand_tilde is the one that matters most: it is what
        -- src/dispatch/trust.rs::project_key keys the trust store on, so
        -- without it the store's path and its key would disagree about an
        -- empty home directory.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Retention: <dispatch_data_dir>/trajectories/<task_id>.jsonl grows unbounded for long-lived tasks. Should there be a size cap, rotation policy, or a wrap-up-time truncation? Currently the file is left untouched for the life of the dispatch installation."

open question "Replay: should a future reader tool reconstruct task state from trajectory.jsonl, or is it write-only audit? The schema_version field is forward-looking but no reader is specified."

open question "Concurrency: when an agent and a runtime path both attempt MCP tool calls for the same task in the same instant, the JSONL append must remain line-atomic. Is single-process open(O_APPEND) sufficient, or is an explicit advisory lock required?"
